.TH EZENV 1 "2026" "ezenv 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ezenv \- opt-in shell environment helpers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ezenv
.I command
.RI [ feature ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ezenv
bundles small, independently enabled zsh helpers. Each feature is a script
you source from your
.IR ~/.zshrc ;
enable some, all, or none.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B enable " [feature...]"
Enable feature(s) by appending a source line to your
.IR ~/.zshrc .
Idempotent. With no feature, enables everything. Run
.B exec zsh
afterward to apply to the current shell.
.RB ( install
is accepted as an alias.)
.TP
.B disable " <feature|all>"
Disable a feature by removing its line from your
.IR ~/.zshrc .
A feature (or
.BR all )
is required \(em a bare
.B disable
will not silently remove everything.
.RB ( uninstall
is accepted as an alias.)
.TP
.B status
Show which features are currently enabled.
.TP
.B doctor
Report the resolved python/pip and the active virtualenv.
.TP
.B init " [feature...]"
Print the raw source line(s) for manual use or
.BR eval .
.TP
.B account " <subcommand>"
Tie directories to different git/ssh accounts. See
.B ACCOUNTS
below.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EZENV_RC
Override the rc file that enable/disable edit (default
.IR ~/.zshrc ).
.TP
.B EZENV_SHIM_DIR
Override where pyf writes its python/pip shims.
.TP
.B EZENV_SSH_CONFIG
Override the ssh config that
.B account
appends host aliases to (default
.IR ~/.ssh/config ).
.TP
.B EZENV_GITCONFIG
Override the git config that
.B account
appends
.I includeIf
stanzas to (default
.IR ~/.gitconfig ).
.TP
.B EZENV_SSH_KEY_DIR
Override where
.B account
generates keypairs (default
.IR ~/.ssh ).
.SH FEATURES
.TP
.B autovenv
Auto-activate the nearest
.I .venv
when you cd into a directory tree that contains one, and deactivate on leaving.
The current directory wins: leaving every
.I .venv
scope deactivates whatever is active, including a venv activated by your editor.
On
.BR "ezenv enable autovenv" ,
it offers (with a prompt) to disable VSCode/Cursor's own terminal venv
auto-activation
.RB ( python.terminal.activateEnvironment )
in your user settings, so autovenv is the sole venv manager;
.B "ezenv disable autovenv"
offers to undo it. Those edits are backed up alongside the file
.RB ( .ezenv-bak ).
.TP
.B pyf
Create shims so bare
.B python
and
.B pip
resolve to python3/pip3, without shadowing virtualenvs or other interpreters.
.TP
.B envup
Add an
.B envup
shell function that exports a
.I .env
file into the current shell \(em
.B envup
loads
.IR ./.env ,
.B "envup FILE"
a specific one. Shorthand for
.BR "set -a; source <file>; set +a" .
It is a sourced function rather than an
.B ezenv
subcommand because a subprocess cannot export variables back into your shell.
Named
.B envup
rather than
.B dotenv
to avoid shadowing the python-dotenv CLI.
.SH ACCOUNTS
Work split across more than one git host account \(em a personal one and an
employer's, say \(em normally means remembering to set
.I user.email
per clone and juggling ssh keys by hand.
.B ezenv account
wires that up once per account and then makes it automatic per directory: every
repo under a bound directory commits as that account and reaches the host with
that account's key.
.PP
Unlike the features above, nothing is sourced into your shell. Git reads
.I includeIf
from your git config and ssh reads the
.I Host
alias from your ssh config on their own, so this is persistent configuration
plus a keypair. ezenv only ever appends, wrapped in
.I BEGIN/END
sentinel comments, and never clobbers an existing key, host block, or identity
file.
.TP
.B account add " <name> --email <email> [--name <git name>] [--host <host>] [--dir <path>]"
Generate an ed25519 keypair, append a
.RI Host " <host>-<name>"
alias, and write an identity file holding the git
.I user.name
and
.IR user.email .
.I --name
defaults to
.IR <name> ,
.I --host
to
.IR github.com .
With
.I --dir
the directory is bound in the same step. Prints the public key to add to the
host account.
.TP
.B account bind " <name> <dir>"
Use that account's identity for
.I <dir>
and everything under it. The directory need not exist yet.
.TP
.B account list
Show each account, its email, and the directories bound to it.
.TP
.B account key " <name>"
Print the account's public key again \(em for pasting into the host, or for
authorizing it against an SSO-enforcing organization.
.TP
.B account check
Verify every account and binding still applies, and exit non-zero if not. This
is the command worth running: a bound directory that is renamed or moved leaves
its
.I includeIf
pointing at a path that no longer exists, so git silently falls back to your
global identity and the wrong name lands on every commit with no error anywhere.
Where a repository exists under a binding,
.B check
confirms end to end that git really does resolve the expected address.
.PP
Keys are generated without a passphrase: this is a convenience tool, and a
passphrase would need agent plumbing on every clone. They are protected by file
permissions only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/ezenv/*.zsh
The feature scripts you source.
.TP
.I ~/.gitconfig-<account>
Per-account git identity, included from
.I ~/.gitconfig
for bound directories.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_<account>
Per-account ssh key.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable features, then reload the shell to apply:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ezenv enable autovenv       # per-repo .venv auto-activation
ezenv enable pyf            # bare python/pip -> python3/pip3
ezenv enable                # everything
exec zsh                    # apply to the current shell
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check and undo:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ezenv status                # what's enabled
ezenv disable pyf           # turn one off
.RE
.fi
.PP
Tie a directory to a second git account:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ezenv account add work --email me@work.com --dir ~/code/work
# paste the printed key into that host account, then:
git clone git@github.com-work:acme/api.git ~/code/work/api
ezenv account check          # confirm it really applies
.RE
.fi
.PP
Wire it up by hand instead (no
.IR ~/.zshrc
edit):
.PP
.nf
.RS
source "$(brew --prefix)/share/ezenv/autovenv.zsh"
source "$(brew --prefix)/share/ezenv/pyf.zsh"
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR brew (1)
